WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:01.540
Hank: Good morning, John.

00:00:01.540 --> 00:00:07.450
In 2012, on September 11th, a group of militants
attacked the US Consulate in Benghazi, Libya.

00:00:07.450 --> 00:00:11.690
Now, three years later, if you live in America,
the word "Benghazi" is either one of those

00:00:11.690 --> 00:00:16.289
words that gets you real riled up, or it's
a word that if you hear it, you're gotta get

00:00:16.289 --> 00:00:20.360
ready to run away from whatever nearby person
is about to get real riled up.

00:00:20.360 --> 00:00:24.890
It's one of those topics that you either know
a whole lot about or you know pretty much

00:00:24.890 --> 00:00:25.890
nothing about.

00:00:25.890 --> 00:00:29.570
And the trouble is, learning about Benghazi
is, no matter what, going to expose you to

00:00:29.570 --> 00:00:31.670
a great deal of partisan anger.

00:00:31.670 --> 00:00:36.340
Figuring out why this is, what actually happened,
what it means, and hopefully, removing it

00:00:36.340 --> 00:00:41.100
a little bit from that crazy, angry partisan
grandstanding is what we're gonna try to do

00:00:41.100 --> 00:00:42.140
in the next seven minutes.

00:00:42.140 --> 00:00:45.780
Let's start out with Benghazi, which despite
the fact that every American has heard of

00:00:45.780 --> 00:00:51.320
it as, at minimum, a site of a tragedy, at
maximum, a site of a massive conspiracy, is

00:00:51.320 --> 00:00:53.030
actually a city in Libya.

00:00:53.030 --> 00:00:54.030
It's big.

00:00:54.030 --> 00:00:56.670
It's about 600,000 people, roughly the size
of Denver, Colorado.

00:00:56.670 --> 00:01:00.460
In 2009, a pro-democracy protest broke out
in Benghazi, which then spread across the

00:01:00.460 --> 00:01:04.850
entire country, and eventually resulted in
the first Libyan Civil War.

00:01:04.850 --> 00:01:05.970
Revolutions are very exciting.

00:01:05.970 --> 00:01:09.690
They promise the possibility of rapid change
for good, and that's exactly what we were

00:01:09.690 --> 00:01:14.530
all hoping for after Muammar Qaddafi, generally
accepted to be a pretty bad dude, was killed

00:01:14.530 --> 00:01:16.020
and his government toppled.

00:01:16.020 --> 00:01:20.140
Unfortunately, the usual immediate outcome
of a revolution is a power vacuum, followed

00:01:20.140 --> 00:01:26.070
by groups of people who want very badly to
control the future of their country with guns.

00:01:26.070 --> 00:01:29.730
There was a great deal of hope that Libya,
and especially Benghazi, where the protests

00:01:29.730 --> 00:01:34.460
first began, would find a quick path to stability,
and depending on who you ask, the Obama Administration

00:01:34.460 --> 00:01:40.340
was either too optimistic about this or criminally
negligent in pretending that it was all okay

00:01:40.340 --> 00:01:44.520
when it really wasn't and they just wanted
to pretend it was for political reasons.

00:01:44.520 --> 00:01:49.000
Let's get this straight: in this story, there
will be two sides to absolutely everything,

00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:52.340
and both of those sides want very badly to
control the future of their country.

00:01:52.340 --> 00:01:57.030
You know, with incessant yammering on cable
news and 85 different congressional committees,

00:01:57.030 --> 00:01:58.280
but thankfully without guns!

00:01:58.280 --> 00:02:02.690
Now, there isn't much controversy about what
actually happened on September 11th, 2012.

00:02:02.690 --> 00:02:06.570
A group of armed militants easily breached
the Consulate, security experts attempted

00:02:06.570 --> 00:02:11.170
to gather all of the personnel and move them
to the safety of a nearby CIA compound that

00:02:11.170 --> 00:02:12.670
was much more easily defensible.

00:02:12.670 --> 00:02:17.050
Two people, Ambassador Chris Stevens and diplomat
Sean Smith, were in a safe room.

00:02:17.050 --> 00:02:20.220
After the compound was lit on fire, both of
them died of smoke inhalation.

00:02:20.220 --> 00:02:23.480
The rest of the Consulate staff was taken
to the CIA compound, which was then later

00:02:23.480 --> 00:02:24.500
attacked as well.

00:02:24.500 --> 00:02:28.790
There, two security officers, both former
Navy Seals, Glen Doherty and Tyrone Woods,

00:02:28.790 --> 00:02:29.959
were killed by mortar fire.

00:02:29.959 --> 00:02:34.160
It was believed that Ambassador Stevens might
still be alive; his body wasn't found at the

00:02:34.160 --> 00:02:37.420
consulate, but it turned out that a group
of Libyans had tried to rescue him, pulled

00:02:37.420 --> 00:02:41.220
him out of the building, and taken him to
a nearby hospital where doctors were unable

00:02:41.220 --> 00:02:42.280
to resuscitate him.

00:02:42.280 --> 00:02:46.280
Some people saw pictures of the local Libyans
trying to help Ambassador Stevens and put

00:02:46.280 --> 00:02:50.840
text on those images saying that he was being
attacked or tortured by those Libyans, just

00:02:50.840 --> 00:02:54.930
to give you an idea of the kind of constant
and deep misinformation we're dealing with

00:02:54.930 --> 00:02:55.930
here.

00:02:55.930 --> 00:02:56.930
So this was an awful thing that happened.

00:02:56.930 --> 00:03:01.540
It was a terrorist attack, it was a tragedy,
and what you think about it depends largely

00:03:01.540 --> 00:03:06.130
on how you feel about President Obama and
then-Secretary of State Hillary Clinton, who

00:03:06.130 --> 00:03:08.100
was, at the time, head of the State Department.

00:03:08.100 --> 00:03:11.880
People predisposed to distrusting the Democratic
leadership tend to believe that they made

00:03:11.880 --> 00:03:16.520
grave mistakes and were criminally negligent
and have so far successfully covered up their

00:03:16.520 --> 00:03:20.090
criminal negligence, while people who find
it easier to trust Clinton and Obama tend

00:03:20.090 --> 00:03:22.061
to accept the State Department's version of
events.

00:03:22.061 --> 00:03:27.070
And that version indeed says that grave mistakes
were made in not preparing the Consulate for

00:03:27.070 --> 00:03:31.340
the instability of the situation and that
they could have been more responsive and that

00:03:31.340 --> 00:03:32.340
they were not.

00:03:32.340 --> 00:03:35.030
But it doesn't go any deeper than that, and
it doesn't go any further up the food chain

00:03:35.030 --> 00:03:36.030
than that.

00:03:36.030 --> 00:03:38.670
Those who distrust the Democratic leadership
say that Clinton and Obama had a vested interest

00:03:38.670 --> 00:03:42.430
in down-playing the severity of the attack,
and yeah, maybe they did.

00:03:42.430 --> 00:03:44.390
Nobody wants to have a terrorist attack on
their record.

00:03:44.390 --> 00:03:48.350
This viewpoint is upheld by the fact that
immediately after the attack, the CIA indicated

00:03:48.350 --> 00:03:53.180
and the Administration repeated that the attack
was just sort of a natural outgrowth of protests

00:03:53.180 --> 00:03:57.240
that were happening because of an anti-Islam
film, when in fact it was definitely a planned

00:03:57.240 --> 00:03:58.860
and organized terrorist attack.

00:03:58.860 --> 00:04:01.129
This was all very close to the 2012 election.

00:04:01.129 --> 00:04:05.450
Obama didn't want to be talking about how
he'd let a terrorist attack happen and it

00:04:05.450 --> 00:04:08.140
was weeks before he even used the word "terrorist".

00:04:08.140 --> 00:04:12.750
But downplaying a crisis after it's happened
is, at least to me, very far away from criminal

00:04:12.750 --> 00:04:13.750
negligence.

00:04:13.750 --> 00:04:17.519
Other accusations like the then-Secretary
Clinton called off troops that could have

00:04:17.519 --> 00:04:21.449
helped have been proved over and over again
to just be made up.

00:04:21.449 --> 00:04:23.460
But the State Department did make mistakes.

00:04:23.460 --> 00:04:27.550
They could have and should have done more
to protect the Consulate, but those shortcomings

00:04:27.550 --> 00:04:30.860
have been acknowledged and indeed they were
acknowledged in the State Department's own

00:04:30.860 --> 00:04:31.860
internal report.

00:04:31.860 --> 00:04:35.910
Since then there have been seven more investigations
that have found nothing new.

00:04:35.910 --> 00:04:40.930
At a certain point, it very clearly went beyond
simple partisan distrust and a desire to get

00:04:40.930 --> 00:04:47.449
to whatever truth is down there and morphed
into an actual active taxpayer-funded campaign

00:04:47.449 --> 00:04:49.830
against the presumptive Democratic nominee
for President.

00:04:49.830 --> 00:04:52.979
Of course, left-wing pundits have been saying
this the whole time, but right-wing pundits

00:04:52.979 --> 00:04:56.270
would then say, "No, we're just trying to
get to the bottom of it" and left-wing pundits

00:04:56.270 --> 00:04:58.830
would be like, "You're at the bottom, there's
nothing there."

00:04:58.830 --> 00:05:00.939
And they would just yell at each other, and
that's how it went for a pretty long time.

00:05:00.939 --> 00:05:05.349
But then, somewhat disturbingly, one of the
staunchest proponents for Benghazi investigations

00:05:05.349 --> 00:05:07.979
came forward on national TV and said this:

00:05:07.979 --> 00:05:11.979
Kevin McCarthy: Everybody thought Hillary
Clinton was unbeatable, right?

00:05:11.979 --> 00:05:15.550
But we put together a Benghazi special committee.

00:05:15.550 --> 00:05:16.750
What are her numbers today?

00:05:16.750 --> 00:05:19.479
Hank: Now a lot of people on both sides were
like "Yeah, of course.

00:05:19.479 --> 00:05:22.259
Of course this is what this is about, it IS
partisan.

00:05:22.259 --> 00:05:26.939
It IS an opportunity for one party to gain
advantage over the other."

00:05:26.939 --> 00:05:29.939
But coming out and proudly saying on national
TV that you're running a series of taxpayer

00:05:29.939 --> 00:05:35.189
funded investigations to advance your political
party, that's bad.

00:05:35.189 --> 00:05:36.189
That's wrong.

00:05:36.189 --> 00:05:40.659
I mean it's wrong to do it in the first place
but, like, you can't say it, and indeed that

00:05:40.659 --> 00:05:44.749
guy who was probably going to become Speaker
of the House, is no longer going to become

00:05:44.749 --> 00:05:46.960
Speaker of the House, 'cause that was dumb.

00:05:46.960 --> 00:05:51.779
Today in America, the controversy surrounding
the Benghazi attack has become so entrenched

00:05:51.779 --> 00:05:55.449
that there are a lot of people whose minds
will never be changed, a lot of people who

00:05:55.449 --> 00:05:59.960
will believe forever that Obama and Clinton
were criminally negligent, and a lot of people

00:05:59.960 --> 00:06:04.939
who will believe forever that it was never
anything more than an opportunity for the

00:06:04.939 --> 00:06:06.530
Republicans to attack the Democrats.

00:06:06.530 --> 00:06:09.800
Now some people absolutely do believe that
there's a prize at the bottom of this Cracker

00:06:09.800 --> 00:06:14.659
Jack box, that the President and Hillary Clinton
are evil and that this is the best chance

00:06:14.659 --> 00:06:16.550
they have of uncovering that evil.

00:06:16.550 --> 00:06:20.439
But eight congressional investigations later,
that's six more than we had after September

00:06:20.439 --> 00:06:25.879
11th 2001, it's seeming pretty unlikely that
we're gonna find out anything, and it's seeming

00:06:25.879 --> 00:06:31.789
more likely that this was a series of unfortunate
circumstances and significant, but not criminal,

00:06:31.789 --> 00:06:32.789
mistakes.

00:06:32.789 --> 00:06:35.949
For most people in politics, though, Benghazi
is now just a talking point.

00:06:35.949 --> 00:06:41.330
It's a way to raise money from increasingly
polarized constituencies and also a way to

00:06:41.330 --> 00:06:43.379
polarize those constituencies more.

00:06:43.379 --> 00:06:46.460
But even as it occasionally takes over U.S.
politics, the attack on the Consulate was

00:06:46.460 --> 00:06:48.169
a much bigger deal for Libya.

00:06:48.169 --> 00:06:52.619
After the attack, aid organizations, investors,
business people, and businesses all left the

00:06:52.619 --> 00:06:53.759
city crushing the economy.

00:06:53.759 --> 00:06:58.159
Other armies and militias saw the attack on
the consulate as brazen and inexcusable and

00:06:58.159 --> 00:07:00.050
they took up arms and fighting increased.

00:07:00.050 --> 00:07:03.889
This led to the eventual second Libyan Civil
War which began in 2014.

00:07:03.889 --> 00:07:08.090
Benghazi now has large areas that are completely
in ruins, and the future of the city, and

00:07:08.090 --> 00:07:10.259
the future of Libya is fearfully in question.

00:07:10.259 --> 00:07:12.860
So that is what's happening in actual Benghazi.

00:07:12.860 --> 00:07:17.129
As we here in America continue to talk about
Benghazi as if it's just a twelve hour period

00:07:17.129 --> 00:07:22.869
of time in 2012 that was, of course, tragic
but ultimately part of a much larger and longer

00:07:22.869 --> 00:07:24.300
series of tragedies.

00:07:24.300 --> 00:07:25.909
John, I'll see you on Tuesday.

00:07:25.909 --> 00:07:30.279
Since it's only two o'clock in the morning
and I just finished editing this video, I

00:07:30.279 --> 00:07:31.870
figured I would talk a little more.

00:07:31.870 --> 00:07:33.580
We've done other videos like this in the past.

00:07:33.580 --> 00:07:36.009
If you want to check those out I'll put a
link to a playlist.

00:07:36.009 --> 00:07:40.499
We've been doing it pretty much since the
beginning of Vlogbrothers, and I like that

00:07:40.499 --> 00:07:45.360
we get to do videos like this, especially
because we get to go past the time limit.

00:07:45.360 --> 00:07:48.300
But it's hard and it's stressful and it's
two o'clock in the morning, and I still have

00:07:48.300 --> 00:07:54.800
to edit this bit, and I don't, I haven't found
many examples of people who are trying to

00:07:54.800 --> 00:07:55.800
do this.

00:07:55.800 --> 00:07:59.550
It seems a little bit like you're not going
to get a ton of views if you're not all about

00:07:59.550 --> 00:08:03.520
outrage and you're not all about what the
other side is doing wrong and how evil they

00:08:03.520 --> 00:08:06.620
are, and so there aren't a lot of people doing
it.

00:08:06.620 --> 00:08:11.530
If I saw someone doing it, I would either
hire them or I would just talk about them

00:08:11.530 --> 00:08:12.530
all the time.

00:08:12.530 --> 00:08:18.050
The question is am I, is it just my own bias
that makes me think that all these other people

00:08:18.050 --> 00:08:19.050
are biased?

00:08:19.050 --> 00:08:24.330
I don't think it is, mostly because the way
to talk about it is from a little bit outside

00:08:24.330 --> 00:08:29.959
of the political system, and nobody seems
interested in doing that because that detachment

00:08:29.959 --> 00:08:32.830
doesn't get people that interested.

00:08:32.830 --> 00:08:36.810
But if you know of people who are writing
about complicated issues in a way that gives

00:08:36.810 --> 00:08:45.120
context, is a more full picture of something
and doesn't, you know, very particularly have

00:08:45.120 --> 00:08:52.740
a goal of promoting the ideology or the agenda
of one party or another, let me know in the

00:08:52.740 --> 00:08:53.740
comments.

00:08:53.740 --> 00:09:00.030
I'd love to read that, 'cause it would be
nice just to help me research these, 'cause

00:09:00.030 --> 00:09:07.370
it's really not fun to read two angry positions
and then try and figure out which parts of

00:09:07.370 --> 00:09:14.700
their anger are real and which parts are just
them being angry, which is the majority of

00:09:14.700 --> 00:09:15.700
it.

00:09:15.700 --> 00:09:18.640
Another nice thing about this being an educational
video, is I just can keep talking, but I'm

00:09:18.640 --> 00:09:19.640
gonna stop now.

00:09:19.640 --> 00:09:24.440
Thank you for watching and I hope that it
was useful.

00:09:24.440 --> 00:09:24.960
And not awful.

